In Python, methods that operate on the class itself rather than an instance are declared using special decorators. A class method receives the class as the first argument, conventionally named cls, and is decorated with @classmethod. A static method does not receive either the instance (self) or the class (cls) automatically; it is decorated with @staticmethod and behaves like a regular function namespaced inside the class. Here is an example:

```python
class Calculator:
    value = 10

    @classmethod
    def show_class_value(cls):
        print('Class value:', cls.value)

    @staticmethod
    def show_static_value():
        print('Static value: 20')
```

Notice that show_static_value does not use cls or self, while show_class_value uses cls to access class attributes.

